Venus on Android with Cuttlefish's minigbm (Mesa 26.1.6, applied by
tools/a64_venus/build.mjs; docs/arm64-virt-android16-plan.md P11.4):

- vn_renderer_virtgpu.c: open the render node by path when drmGetDevices2
  fails. Android's SELinux policy lets apps, SurfaceFlinger and gpuservice
  open /dev/dri/renderD128 (gralloc opens it in every process) but not list
  /dev/dri or read the device's sysfs, which drmGetDevices2 needs; the
  driver then finds no device and reports none, silently.
- u_gralloc_minigbm.c: minigbm's buffer handles read directly (its packed
  cros_gralloc_handle, recognised by its magic). Cuttlefish's minigbm has the
  AIDL allocator and IMapper5 but no gralloc0 module for the CrOS API, and
  the NDK build has no libui for IMapper; the fallback backend needs a pixel
  stride and HAL format venus does not give it, so AHardwareBuffers could
  not be imported.
- vn_physical_device.c: on Android, dma-buf imports (AHardwareBuffers) with
  a renderer that has the modifier and foreign queue extensions but not
  VK_EXT_external_memory_dma_buf: v86's imports virtio-gpu resources
  (VkImportMemoryResourceInfoMESA) and makes no dma-bufs, and advertising
  the extension would put venus on Linux on dma-buf swapchain images.
  vn_device.c then does not ask such a renderer for the dma-buf extensions.
- vn_common.c: on Android no abort when a wait outlives the ring's ALIVE
  reports (VN_DEBUG=no_abort): v86's renderer runs on the host's main
  thread, which can stop for seconds while the guest's time goes on;
  Launcher3 aborted so in a run. A ring's FATAL status still aborts.

